      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, why are you talking about goblins and wizards? I'm literally just asking about aliasing in signal processing. It's a math problem, not a story.\n\nCan we please stick to the actual mechanics? You mentioned the spectrum being \"reflected back.\" Can you show me the mathematical derivation of how the periodic summation of the spectrum leads to these overlapping \"alias\" frequencies? I need to see the actual math, not the \"mischievous goblin\" metaphor.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's dive straight into the mathematics of aliasing. When you sample a continuous-time signal at a rate less than twice its highest frequency component (the Nyquist rate), the spectrum of the signal is folded over. This folding is a result of the periodic nature of the sampled signal's Fourier transform.\n\nConsider a continuous-time signal x(t) with a Fourier transform X(f). When you sample this signal at a sampling frequency fs, the sampled signal x_s(t) = x(t) * s(t), where s(t) is the sampling function, typically a rectangular pulse of duration T = 1/fs. The Fourier transform of the sampled signal, X_s(f), is given by the convolution of X(f) with the Fourier transform of the sampling function, S(f).\n\nThe Fourier transform of the sampling function, S(f), is a series of impulses spaced fs apart in the frequency domain. Mathematically, this can be represented as:\n\nS(f) = ∑_{k=-∞}^{∞} δ(f - kfs)\n\nwhere δ(f) is the Dirac delta function. The Fourier transform of the sampled signal, X_s(f), is then:\n\nX_s(f) = X(f) * S(f",
      "source": "target",
